course: dictionary
lesson: 9104
title: Smallpox and the clues around it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Smallpox is a noun, the name of a serious disease. You may not know this word, but the words around it give you clues. «Smallpox was a very serious, catching disease that caused a high fever and spots that turned into blisters.» A fever, spots and blisters all tell you it is an illness, and catching tells you it spreads from person to person.
explain: Look at what smallpox did. «Smallpox often left deep scars or killed people.» Deep scars and killing people tell you smallpox was very dangerous. Always ask: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Read a longer sentence from history. «Diseases such as smallpox, carried by Europeans, killed very many native people of the Americas.» The words carried by and killed show that smallpox moved between people and caused great harm. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Smallpox is a noun. It is a name for a disease.
fact: Smallpox is uncountable: you say smallpox, not smallpoxes.
fact: Smallpox was a very serious, catching disease with a high fever and spots that turned into blisters.
fact: Smallpox is the first human disease ever wiped out completely from the world, thanks to vaccination.
fact: A vaccine shows the body a harmless copy or part of a germ, so the body learns to fight it. Edward Jenner made the first vaccine in 1796, from cowpox.
exercise: «Smallpox was a very serious, catching disease.» What is smallpox? | A kind of sweet made with sugar | A tall stone tower on a hill | A sport played on ice | *A serious, catching disease
exercise: «Smallpox often left deep scars or killed people.» What does this tell you? | It was harmless and easy to cure | It only made people sneeze | It was a funny kind of food | *It was very dangerous
exercise: Smallpox was wiped out. What does «wiped out» mean? | Moved to a new place | Painted over with a thick coat | *Destroyed so completely it no longer exists | Cleaned with a cloth
test: Which disease is the first human disease ever wiped out completely? | *Smallpox | Measles | Mumps | Flu
test: What does a vaccine do? | Makes the body grow taller very fast | Stops all sunlight from reaching the skin | Turns spots into blisters | *Shows the body a harmless copy of a germ so it can learn to fight it
exam: What does «vacca», the Latin word that gives us vaccine, mean? | *Cow | Germ | Dog | Horse
